cosity of glass at room temperature is estimated to be around 10²⁰ to 10²⁴ poise (for comparison, water is 0.01 poise, honey is ~100 poise). This is so astronomically high that if you ran the math: - It would take **trillions of years or longer** for a window pane to show any measurable sag—far, far longer than the age of the universe (~13.8 billion years). - In the 500–1,000 years that some cathedrals have existed, the flow would be practically zero, on the order of thousands of times smaller than the width of an atom. So, the myth fails both because glass isn't a liquid and because, even if it were, the timeline isn't nearly long enough. ### What Really Explains the Thick Bottoms of Old Windows? Art history and manufacturing, not physics. In the past, window glass was made by two main methods: - **Crown glass:** A blown glass bubble was spun into a large, flat disk. This disk would be thicker at the center and thinner at the edges. When cut into panes, the pieces were naturally uneven. - **Cylinder glass:** A blown cylinder was cut and flattened, yielding sheets with slight ripples and thickness variations. Glaziers, being practical, tended to install the panes with the heavier, thicker edge at the bottom for stability and to prevent water from pooling against the lead cames. It wasn’t flow—it was just smart craftsmanship. So, rest assured, the stunning stained-glass windows of Chartres or Notre-Dame are frozen in place, not in motion. They’re a fascinating testament to medieval artistry and the solid nature of glass.
